FOOTNOTES:

[Footnote 1:

Next came Anarchy, he rode

On a white horse splashed with blood;

He was pale even to the lips,

Like Death in the Apocalypse.

]

[[Footnote 2: N.B.—The camel recovered.]]

[[Footnote 3: For my memorandum, see Appendix II.]]

[[Footnote 4: Afterwards Ambassador at Constantinople.]]

[[Footnote 5: Ismaïl Bey Jowdat, director of the Cairo police under the Nationalist Government in 1882. See my volumes, “Secret History” and “Gordon at Khartoum.”]]

[[Footnote 6: Sir Arthur Hardinge, then Secretary of Legation at Cairo, afterwards our Minister at Brussels.]]

[[Footnote 7: Lionel Lord Sackville, formerly of the Diplomatic Service, and Her Majesty’s Minister at Washington.]]

[[Footnote 8: For Seyyid Jemal ed Din Afghani’s earlier career and his visit to me in London see my volume, “Gordon at Khartoum.” See also Professor Browne’s account of the Seyyid in his book on Persia.]]

[[Footnote 9: See “Secret History.”]]

[[Footnote 10: This Osman Ghaleb became afterwards the principal friend and supporter of the National Leader, Mustapha Kamel.]]

[Footnote 11: Not long ago, being asked to write a sonnet for the
Shakespeare Tercentenary I embodied my impression gathered on this
occasion at Stratford in the following:

“A TERCENTENARY SONNET

“SHAKESPEARE, what wisdom shall truth tell of thee,

More than fame speaks? The world thy playhouse is

Packed floor to roof to-night with votaries

Shouting thy author’s name vociferously.

They call thee to the curtain front. Ah me,

Hast thou no word for our sublimities,

No cryptogram of grace to crown our bliss?

Nay speak out all, thou man of mystery.

Tell us the truth.—I seem to hear a voice

From far-off Stratford, pestered at the call,

The voice of a hale man of middle age,

Civic, respected: ‘Who are these lewd boys

Would call me back to their fool’s festival?

Truce to all mummings. I have left the stage.’”

_25 February_, 1916.]

[[Footnote 12: Malta had been granted a Constitution of very restricted type by the English Government in 1887, avowedly as an experiment, with the result that many abuses in the government of the island were remedied; but a strong movement having been set on foot by the native Maltese for union with the Italian kingdom the Constitution was subsequently withdrawn.]]

[[Footnote 13: Mohammed Rashid, afterwards Sultan Mohammed V.]]

[[Footnote 14: See “Secret History.”]]

[[Footnote 15: Compare Dr. Dillon’s “Eclipse of Russia.”]]

[[Footnote 16: A fortnight or three weeks before Beiram.]]

[[Footnote 17: Compare Dr. Dillon’s “Eclipse of Russia.”]]

[[Footnote 18: John Henry Middleton, director of the South Kensington Museum.]]

[[Footnote 19: Now Lord Eversley.]]

[[Footnote 20: Lord Hampden.]]

[[Footnote 21: Compare Dr. Dillon’s book, “The Eclipse of Russia.”]]

[[Footnote 22: “Times,” 1900.]]

[[Footnote 23: The Honble. Henry Howard, Secretary at Paris.]]

